# applications/knowledge/platform/env/_applications/knowledge/base.env
#
# The Application scope: every Process of `knowledge` receives these
# (docs/adr/model/0124-shared-intent-descends-to-the-process.md). Nine of the
# twelve lines each Process file used to carry were byte-identical between them,
# which is the duplication `_applications/` exists to end. Both Processes reach
# the same Postgres and the same RabbitMQ, so both read the same coordinates and
# the same credentials.
#
# A Process that needed a different value for one of these would set it in its
# own file, where a reader sees it; setting it to the SAME value there is
# E_SHARED_DECLARATION_DUPLICATED, because the line would change nothing.

# --- coordinates, from the dependsOn edges both Processes declare
DB_HOST=${dependency:platform-postgres.host}
DB_PORT=${dependency:platform-postgres.port}
DB_NAME=${dependency:platform-postgres.database}
RABBITMQ_HOST=${dependency:platform-rabbitmq.host}
RABBITMQ_PORT=${dependency:platform-rabbitmq.port}

# --- secrets, each byte-matching a path the Application grants
#     (docs/adr/model/0027-secret-reference-join-key.md). The grants sit on the
#     Application too, one level up from the Processes that hold them, so the
#     grant and the reference that binds it are written at the same level.
DB_USER=${secret:secret/data/platform/postgres/kb#user}
DB_PASSWORD=${secret:secret/data/platform/postgres/kb#password}
RABBITMQ_USER=${secret:secret/data/platform/rabbitmq#rabbitmq.user}
RABBITMQ_PASSWORD=${secret:secret/data/platform/rabbitmq#rabbitmq.password}
